---
title: "SDK overview"
description: "Choose the qql-rs Rust, Python, Node.js, or WebAssembly integration."
sidebar:
  order: 1
---

All SDKs use the same language and plan model, but host capabilities differ.

| Host | Package | Parser | Live execution | Edge |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Rust | `qql-core`, `qql` | yes | REST + gRPC | separate crate |
| Python | `pyqql` | yes | REST + gRPC | `pyqql-edge` |
| Node.js | `@veristamp/nqql` | yes | REST + gRPC | `@veristamp/nqql-edge` |
| Browser/WASM | `qql-wasm` | yes | REST/fetch | no |

## Shared concepts

- `parse` returns typed or host-owned statement objects.
- `is_valid` / `isValid` performs strict full-input parsing.
- `inject_filter` / `injectFilter` rewrites the AST.
- `compile` and `explain` are offline planning tools.
- `execute` accepts a statement or semicolon-delimited script.
- Host arrays represent independent inputs.

{% qqlExample title="Shared SDK input" %}
QUERY TEXT 'portable query' FROM docs
USING dense
LIMIT 5;
{% /qqlExample %}

The SDK pages use `query` to refer to the verified statement above, keeping the
language example separate from host syntax.
